      <h1 class="narrative-title">Superfund Sites & WA State Environmental Cleanups</h1>
      <p class="narrative-lede">
        Navigating federal and state cleanup zones across King and Snohomish counties: EPA Superfund projects, Washington Model Toxics Control Act (MTCA) database tools, and property due diligence.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Real Estate Coach Callout -->
    <div class="coach-callout">
      <h3>💡 Real Estate Coach's Take</h3>
      <p>
        Environmental cleanup tracking is actually one of the greatest tools home buyers and sellers have in Washington State. First, a vital fact: <strong>industrial river and commercial site cleanups have zero impact on your home's indoor drinking water</strong>, which is piped directly from protected, high-elevation mountain watersheds (the Cedar and Tolt rivers in King County, and Spada Lake in Snohomish County). Second, Washington operates one of the most transparent environmental tracking systems in the nation. Between federal EPA Superfund oversight and the state Department of Ecology's Model Toxics Control Act (MTCA) database, you can search any address in minutes to verify historical commercial uses, former dry cleaners or gas stations, and completed soil remedies.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Body Section -->
    <section class="narrative-section">
      <h2>Federal Superfund Sites vs. State MTCA Cleanups</h2>
      <p>
        Environmental cleanup sites in Western Washington generally fall under two distinct regulatory programs depending on the size, history, and severity of the location:
      </p>

      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Cleanup Program</th>
            <th>Governing Agency</th>
            <th>Scope & Typical Examples</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Federal Superfund (CERCLA)</strong></td>
            <td>U.S. EPA Region 10</td>
            <td>Large-scale industrial waterways and historic manufacturing hubs (Lower Duwamish Waterway, Harbor Island, Commencement Bay).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>State MTCA Cleanup Sites</strong></td>
            <td>WA Department of Ecology</td>
            <td>Local commercial properties, former gas stations, dry cleaners, or industrial yards cleaned up independently or under state supervision.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>1. Federal Superfund Sites: The Lower Duwamish Waterway</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>Lower Duwamish Waterway Superfund Site</strong> encompasses a 5.5-mile stretch of the river flowing north through South Park, Georgetown, and Tukwila into Elliott Bay. Listed by the EPA in 2001, this industrial corridor is undergoing extensive multi-agency restoration:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Riverbed Sediment Remediation:</strong> Involves targeted dredging of high-priority toxic spots, capping riverbed areas with engineered layers of clean sand and gravel, and monitored natural recovery.</li>
        <li><strong>Source Control:</strong> The WA Department of Ecology works alongside local municipalities to inspect industrial storm drains and prevent re-contamination.</li>
        <li><strong>Resident Seafood Harvest Advisories:</strong> While migratory salmon pass through safely, state health authorities advise against eating resident bottom-dwelling fish (perch, sole) or crabs harvested directly from the river.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2. WA Department of Ecology "What's in My Neighborhood" Database</h2>
      <p>
        Beyond large federal Superfund zones, the Washington Department of Ecology maintains an interactive public mapping database called <strong>What's in My Neighborhood</strong>. This portal maps thousands of localized environmental sites across King and Snohomish counties:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Active Cleanup Sites:</strong> Properties currently undergoing environmental assessment, soil removal, or groundwater monitoring under the Model Toxics Control Act (MTCA).</li>
        <li><strong>No Further Action (NFA) Sites:</strong> Properties where soil or groundwater contamination was successfully excavated or remediated to state standards, earning formal state closure certification.</li>
        <li><strong>Voluntary Cleanup Program (VCP):</strong> Independent property owner cleanups conducted with formal technical review and confirmation from Department of Ecology toxicologists.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

